From 354bc8bfa8cbaa2a85eba2d9b5b9974a5052499a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?J=C3=B3n=20Bjarni=20Bjarnason?= <jon@centroid.is>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 16:26:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vnc: null-check the cursor state before using it

vnc_output_update_cursor() dereferences two pointers unchecked that its
sibling vnc_output_assign_cursor_plane() guards:

  pointer          - vnc_output_get_pointer() returns NULL when the first
                     peer's seat has no pointer, or when its sprite is not
                     in the output's z-order list. A newly connected client
                     hits the second case: peers are inserted at the head of
                     output->peers, so it immediately becomes the peer that
                     vnc_output_get_pointer() picks, while no sprite has
                     been set for its seat yet.

  cursor_surface   - output->cursor_surface is assigned in exactly one
                     place, vnc_output_assign_cursor_plane(), and is never
                     cleared. It is NULL until that first succeeds.

The two are refreshed only while vnc_output_assign_planes() keeps calling
vnc_output_assign_cursor_plane(), and it stops doing so as soon as any
connected client does not advertise RFB_ENCODING_CURSOR, since
vnc_clients_support_cursor() then returns false. vnc_output_repaint()
calls vnc_output_update_cursor() unconditionally regardless.

That asymmetry leaves a window in which the reader keeps running while
nothing refreshes what it reads. Observed under gdb on 16.0.0 with two
clients: once a second client without RFB_ENCODING_CURSOR joins, the
assignment stops and the dereference still runs several more times on a
pointer nothing updates any more.

This has not been tied to an observed crash; it is a defensive change to
match the guards the sibling function already applies.
---
 libweston/backend-vnc/vnc.c | 23 +++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libweston/backend-vnc/vnc.c b/libweston/backend-vnc/vnc.c
index 6d3f6748..107a5c76 100644
--- a/libweston/backend-vnc/vnc.c
+++ b/libweston/backend-vnc/vnc.c
@@ -596,8 +596,31 @@ vnc_output_update_cursor(struct vnc_output *output)
 	if (!update_cursor)
 		return;
 
+	/*
+	 * vnc_output_get_pointer() returns NULL when the first peer's seat has
+	 * no pointer, or when the pointer's sprite is not in the output's
+	 * z-order list — which is the case for a client that has just
+	 * connected, since peers are inserted at the head of the list and no
+	 * sprite has been set for its seat yet.
+	 *
+	 * output->cursor_surface is only ever assigned, in
+	 * vnc_output_assign_cursor_plane(), and never cleared. It is NULL
+	 * until that first runs, and afterwards it is refreshed only while
+	 * vnc_output_assign_planes() keeps calling that function — which stops
+	 * as soon as any connected client does not advertise
+	 * RFB_ENCODING_CURSOR. This function is called unconditionally from
+	 * vnc_output_repaint() either way.
+	 *
+	 * vnc_output_assign_cursor_plane() already guards both of these; do
+	 * the same here rather than dereference them unchecked.
+	 */
+	if (!pointer || !output->cursor_surface)
+		return;
+
 	cursor_surface = output->cursor_surface;
 	buffer = cursor_surface->buffer_ref.buffer;
+	if (!buffer)
+		return;
 
 	frame = nvnc_frame_new(buffer->width, buffer->height, DRM_FORMAT_ARGB8888, buffer->width);
 	assert(frame);
